			"notes": "- A cobweb held on two or more sides, by walls, floors, posts or more web, is drawn strung between them: a sheet across a corner, or one web carried on into the next.\n- Now and then a spider hunting something five to fourteen blocks off rears back and throws a ball of web trailing a strand of silk, which leaves a cobweb where it lands that comes apart five seconds later.\n- Now and then an idle spider turns its back to somewhere dark it can string a web across and spins one, strands of silk shooting from it to whatever the web will hang from, beside a web already there when it can, until the place around it has eight.\n- Spiders come in sizes: most smaller than before and no two alike, cave spiders still the smaller kind, and about one in twenty five a big one that is slower and takes two and a half times the hits."
		}
